  "title": "The flitter lands",
  "question": "Does it read as an animal that flies when it has somewhere to go, and waits when it does not?",
  "context_md": "A flitter used to fly whenever it could *see* a flower — and its eye reaches thirty-two cells, so on a bed with flowers in it that was nearly always. It hardly ever came down: **fifty-five per cent of everything the colony ate went on staying in the air, and twenty-nine of its thirty deaths happened in mid-air with a full bed underneath it**.\n\n**Now it flies when a flower is near enough to be worth the trip, and otherwise it lands and sits.** Near enough is about nine cells on a full stomach and about one and a half on half a tank — a hungry animal only lifts for a flower it is practically standing at. Sitting costs a fortieth of what a step costs, and a grounded flitter lives eight thousand frames on what it was born with.\n\n**What it bought** (the bed with the flowering understory, three seeds, 120,000 frames): deaths in mid-air **97/61/37% → 8/10/6%**, young born **5 → 10**, and the longest a colony lasted **24,000 → 48,000 frames**. Chosen out of a four-setting sweep across two beds.\n\n**What it did not buy, and I want to be straight about it: they still all die.** Nobody is alive at 120,000 frames at any setting I tried. They now starve on the ground instead of in the air, and they reach a flower about a fifth as often as the arithmetic says they need to. **So the next thing to fix is what this animal does when there is nothing in sight** — it walks about at ten times the cost of sitting still — rather than anything about its flying.",
